          "title": "The Ancient Art of Kalamkari",
          "excerpt": "Discover the centuries-old tradition of hand-painting myths and motifs on fabric using natural dyes.",
          "body": "Kalamkari means “pen work”. Two living traditions exist in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana. At Srikalahasti the artist draws freehand with a bamboo pen (kalam) and fills the colour in by hand. At Machilipatnam carved wooden blocks print the pattern.\n\nThe cloth is first soaked in a mix of buffalo milk and myrobalan (harada) so that it holds the dye. The colours come from plants and minerals: indigo for blue, madder for red, myrobalan and pomegranate rind for yellow and green, and iron acetate for black. Each colour needs its own wash and drying, so one piece can take weeks.\n\nCare: wash gently in cold water, dry in shade, and expect the natural dyes to soften a little with time. That is part of its character.",
          "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1602161490280-093915e84362?q=80&w=600",
          "alt": "Kalamkari",
          "show": true
        },
